{"qid":"Q37","names":["Republic of Lithuania"],"title":"Republic of Lithuania","endonym":{"native":"Lietuva","lang":"lt"},"capsule":"Lithuania restored its statehood on 11 March 1990, when the Supreme Soviet of the Lithuanian SSR voted to re-establish the independent republic first proclaimed in 1918—becoming the first Soviet republic to declare independence from the USSR. Soviet economic blockade and military force (the January 1991 attacks on the Vilnius TV Tower and parliament) failed to reverse the declaration, and Moscow recognised Lithuanian independence on 6 September 1991 following the failed August coup. Lithuania joined NATO and the European Union in 2004, and since Russia's full-scale invasion of Ukraine in 2022 has emerged as one of the most active advocates for Ukrainian defence within the Alliance.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Lithuania's boundaries from 1990 onward are precisely defined by Soviet administrative borders accepted as international frontiers upon independence; no territorial disputes affect the main body of the state.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Lithuania","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lithuania"},{"label":"Britannica: Lithuania","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Lithuania"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Act of the Re-Establishment of the State of Lithuania","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Act_of_the_Re-Establishment_of_the_State_of_Lithuania"}],"britannica":"place/Lithuania","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Lithuania","rulers":[{"qid":"Q257779","name":"Kazimira Prunskienė","start":1990,"end":1991,"image":"Kazimira Prunskienė 2010.jpg","imageUrl":"h